{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.RothRationalIndex","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.RothRationalIndex","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.RothAuxiliaryConstruction"],"declarations":7,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.le_index_of_abs_lt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.le_index_of_abs_lt","kind":"theorem","line":240,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothRationalIndex.lean#L240-L253","doc":"**Step 2, arithmetic half: the index at the rational point is large.**  Every translated\ncoefficient of weight below `μ` that is small enough vanishes, and enough vanishing is a lower\nbound on the index.  The smallness hypothesis is the analytic half, supplied elsewhere. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.coeff_translate_eq_zero_of_abs_lt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.coeff_translate_eq_zero_of_abs_lt","kind":"theorem","line":221,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothRationalIndex.lean#L221-L238","doc":"**Small forces vanishing.**  A translated coefficient at the rational point that is strictly\nbelow `1/∏ q_h^{r_h}` in absolute value is exactly `0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_int_mul_coeff_translate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_int_mul_coeff_translate","kind":"theorem","line":188,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothRationalIndex.lean#L188-L219","doc":"**A translated coefficient at a rational point has denominator dividing `∏ q_h^{r_h}`.**  The\ndegree bound is what converts the per-monomial scaling of `exists_int_mul_translateMatrix` into a\nsingle scaling valid for the whole polynomial. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.translateMatrix_half_witness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.translateMatrix_half_witness","kind":"theorem","line":175,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothRationalIndex.lean#L175-L186","doc":"**The scaling of `exists_int_mul_translateMatrix` is attained.**  At `β = 1/2` the translated\nconstant term of `x²` is `1/4`, so the `∏ q_h^{n_h}` really is needed and cannot be lowered. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_int_mul_translateMatrix","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_int_mul_translateMatrix","kind":"theorem","line":91,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothRationalIndex.lean#L91-L173","doc":"**The translate matrix at a rational point has a controlled denominator.**  Scaling by\n`∏ q_h^{n_h}` clears it — proved by the same peeling as the height bound, because each linear\nfactor `X_j + p_j/q_j` contributes exactly one `q_j`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_int_sum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_int_sum","kind":"theorem","line":77,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothRationalIndex.lean#L77-L89","doc":"A finite sum of rationals that are each integers is an integer. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.eq_zero_of_abs_lt_of_den","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.eq_zero_of_abs_lt_of_den","kind":"theorem","line":55,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothRationalIndex.lean#L55-L75","doc":"**Small with a bounded denominator forces zero.**  The mechanism of every \"the index at the\nrational point is large\" argument: a rational whose denominator divides `D` is either `0` or at\nleast `1/D` in absolute value. ","signatureChunk":0}]}